blocks.body:
Example: the CLI answers with an envelope, and the model text sits in its "result" field:

{"result": "Here it is: {\"root\": \"...\"} hope this helps"}

The first { and the last } cut the object out of that text, so chatter around it does no harm. A non-zero exit code or a run longer than 600 seconds stops the program. Every try of the retry dance comes through here, and the counter in CALLS makes the number of calls known at the end.

Example: the blocks of this file include ask_claude, compile_block and walk.

vocabulary = [
  {"term": "the retry dance", "meaning": "up to 8 tries of (ask_claude), each retry told which rule broke"},
  {"term": "the gate",        "meaning": "the asserts the retry dance is checked against"}
]

Both meanings pass: (ask_claude) is a real block name, and the edge from "the gate" down to "the retry dance" closes no circle; a name in parentheses that is not a real block name fails here. A dotted word is judged only when its first part is a block name, so ast.parse goes by untouched while the scope path compile_block.foo is caught. If the meaning of "the retry dance" named "the gate" back, the depth-first walk (walk) prints the circle and the vocabulary pass dies.

Example: compile_block is defined on line 337 and used on line 407, inside main. owner turns both lines into block ids, so one row comes out:

{"name": "compile_block", "def": the id of compile_block's own block, "used": [the id of main's block]}

A name that jedi resolves to another module, like json, is dropped, and a file that is not Python gives [] at once. A name used only inside the block that defines it makes no row. Only top-level names are scanned, so a nested block like (resolve) never becomes a row of its own.
